.TE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
Other substitutions are provided that are variations on this base set and
further substitution patterns can be defined by each test module. See the
modules LOCAL CONFIGURATION FILES\&.
.sp
More detailed information on substitutions can be found in the
LLVM Testing Infrastructure Guide \%<>\&.
.SS TEST RUN OUTPUT FORMAT
.sp
The \fBlit\fP output for a test run conforms to the following schema, in
both short and verbose modes (although in short mode no PASS lines will be
shown).  This schema has been chosen to be relatively easy to reliably parse by
a machine (for example in buildbot log scraping), and for other tools to
generate.
.sp
Each test result is expected to appear on a line that matches:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
<result code>: <test name> (<progress info>)
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
where \fB<result\-code>\fP is a standard test result such as PASS, FAIL, XFAIL,
XPASS, UNRESOLVED, or UNSUPPORTED.  The performance result codes of IMPROVED and
REGRESSED are also allowed.
.sp
The \fB<test name>\fP field can consist of an arbitrary string containing no
newline.
.sp
The \fB<progress info>\fP field can be used to report progress information such
as (1/300) or can be empty, but even when empty the parentheses are required.
.sp
Should a test be allowed retries (see \fBALLOW_RETRIES:\fP annotation) and it
needed more than one attempt to succeed, then \fB<progress info>\fP is extended
by this information:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
, <num_attempts_made> of <max_allowed_attempts> attempts
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
Each test result may include additional (multiline) log information in the
following format:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
<log delineator> TEST \(aq(<test name>)\(aq <trailing delineator>
\&... log message ...
<log delineator>
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
where \fB<test name>\fP should be the name of a preceding reported test, \fB<log
delineator>\fP is a string of \(dq*\(dq characters \fIat least\fP four characters long
(the recommended length is 20), and \fB<trailing delineator>\fP is an arbitrary
(unparsed) string.
.sp
The following is an example of a test run output which consists of four tests A,
B, C, and D, and a log message for the failing test C:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
PASS: A (1 of 4)
PASS: B (2 of 4)
FAIL: C (3 of 4)
******************** TEST \(aqC\(aq FAILED ********************
Test \(aqC\(aq failed as a result of exit code 1.
********************
PASS: D (4 of 4)
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.SS DEFAULT FEATURES
.sp
For convenience \fBlit\fP automatically adds \fBavailable_features\fP for
some common use cases.
.sp
\fBlit\fP adds a feature based on the operating system being built on, for
example: \fIsystem\-darwin\fP, \fIsystem\-linux\fP, etc. \fBlit\fP also
automatically adds a feature based on the current architecture, for example
\fItarget\-x86_64\fP, \fItarget\-aarch64\fP, etc.
.sp
When building with sanitizers enabled, \fBlit\fP automatically adds the
short name of the sanitizer, for example: \fIasan\fP, \fItsan\fP, etc.
.sp
To see the full list of features that can be added, see
\fIllvm/utils/lit/lit/llvm/config.py\fP\&.
.SS LIT EXAMPLE TESTS
.sp
The \fBlit\fP distribution contains several example implementations of
test suites in the \fIExampleTests\fP directory.
